WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:02.340
Hello, everyone. My
name is Ryan Johnson,

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:04.920
and I'm a data science
advisor here at Posit.

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:08.220
And welcome to this
month's enterprise community meetup,

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:11.580
where we'll discuss another end
to end data science workflow

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:12.840
using Posit Team.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:14.220
As a reminder,

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:17.400
this is a recurring event
on the last Wednesday of every

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:20.940
single month And its goal is
to provide the data science

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:23.400
community with an
overview of Posit Team,

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:26.760
especially for those that
may be unfamiliar with our

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.820
professional tools or maybe for
those that are looking for a

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:33.420
refresher or hoping to
explore new capabilities and features

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:34.380
within Posit Team.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.260
Every month, we'll
cover a different topic,

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:41.340
and we're gonna highlight all
three tools from within Posit

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:43.860
Team. We have Posit
Workbench, Posit Connect,

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:45.840
and Posit Package Manager.

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:48.840
During and after
each presentation,

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:52.980
members of the Posit Team will
be in the chat to address any

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:54.900
questions you have
about our tools,

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.180
workflows, licensing questions,

00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:59.400
and anything else you
may be curious about.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:01.680
So for this month's topic,

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:05.520
we're gonna take a deep dive
into one of our newest open

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:07.920
source tools called Quarto.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.200
Quarto is a technical
publishing system,

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:12.900
and in many ways it's
similar to R Markdown,

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:16.320
but there's no dependency
on the R programming language.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:20.880
So if you are a Python,
observable, or Julia developer,

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:23.040
you can also create
a Quarto document.

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:25.260
Now if you're new to Quarto,

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:28.140
you are absolutely in the right
place as we are going to create

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:32.160
a brand new quarto document
from scratch. Using the RStudio

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:34.080
IDE within Posit Workbench.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:38.940
We'll then show how Quarto
can be using combination with Posit

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:42.600
Connect to fuel some
really extremely powerful workflows.

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:45.120
Now before we get started,

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:49.050
I just want to quickly
review Posit Team,

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:51.780
and we'll start right here
at the top with your data

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:54.720
scientists and your data
analyst who will be creating

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:57.360
insights using Posit Workbench.

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:01.260
And they can choose to write
code in either R or Python,

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:03.900
and they have a variety
of IDEs to choose from,

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:07.080
including RStudio,
JupyterLabs, Jupyter

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:08.640
Notebooks, and VS Code.

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:11.220
Now for the R developers,

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:14.820
they may be creating
content such as shiny applications,

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:17.160
pins, R Markdown

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:19.020
or plumber APIs.

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:21.360
And for Python developers,

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:24.900
they may be creating content
such as Flask or Fast APIs,

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:29.220
streamlit, shiny, dash
applications, pins, Jupyter notebooks.

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:32.460
Now once the
content is created,

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:37.320
the developers will need a way
to easily share that content with viewers.

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:40.380
And that's where Posit
Connect comes into play.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:43.560
Connect not only makes
your content shareable,

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:46.980
but it also gives your
team tight control over content

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:49.920
access and can also be
used for job scheduling,

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:51.060
which we're gonna
talk about today.

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:55.080
Finally, we have
Posit package manager.

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:58.980
Which does exactly as this
name implies, and that's to host,

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:02.880
organize, and distribute open
source R and Python packages.

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:04.560
Not only from the community,

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:08.520
but also any internally
developed R and Python packages.

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:12.420
Now for today's workflow,

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:17.040
we're going to use the
rstudio IDE from within Posit workbench

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:22.020
to extract and save some
data as a pin. And then we'll

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:25.380
create some insights from
that pinned data using quarto,

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:29.520
which will then publish to Posit Connect
and then run it on a schedule.

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:33.300
So to get started,

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:37.020
let's first talk about the
data we'll be analyzing today.

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:40.920
And I thought it might be
interesting to extract some r

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:45.180
package download data from
within Posit package manager.

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:50.940
And we can do this via
an experimental API from within package manager,

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:53.100
which can be used to
gather lots of great information

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:55.740
including package
download metrics.

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:58.980
So this is great for
auditing purposes,

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:03.240
or maybe you're just curious to
know how popular is a certain

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:07.020
package from within your
instance of Posit package manager.

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:10.680
For example, here's some
example data down here.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:14.400
Of what can be gleaned from
the package manager API.

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:19.980
On the left hand, we can see
the r package name in this case ggplot2.

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:23.760
And then the number of
downloads of this package on a

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:24.420
specific day.

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:28.560
Now this data is
constantly updating.

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:32.100
People continue to download
packages from Posit package manager.

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:35.700
So one of the goals for
today is to automate the data

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:37.680
extraction and
visualization steps,

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:41.820
so that our final reports will
reflect the latest and greatest data.

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:47.160
And that brings us to
quarto. So for today's demo,

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:50.160
we're going to create
two quarto documents.

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:52.320
One will be for gathering,

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:55.020
processing, and then
saving our data.

00:04:55.020 --> 00:05:00.060
This is often referred to as
an ETL or extract transform load workflow.

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:04.740
The other quarto document will
be to generate a report with

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:07.020
tables and plots to
visualize our results.

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:10.020
But what exactly is quarto?

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:14.280
Well, quarto, first and
foremost, is an open source

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:17.760
tool. So if you have a
computer and you have Internet access,

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:20.100
you can create a
quarto document.

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:24.360
It's perfect for scientific
and technical publishing

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:28.620
and can really be thought as
like a next generation of r

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:32.100
markdown. Or our way of
bringing our mark down to everyone.

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:36.900
And since there's no dependency
on the r programming language,

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:40.260
you can create a Quarto document
if you're an r developer,

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:43.620
Python, Julia, or
Observable programmer.

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:47.700
You can also create quarto
documents in whatever

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:51.420
environment is most comfortable for
you, including vs code,

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:53.580
RStudio, which is
where we'll be today,

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:56.400
JupyterLab or any text editor.

00:05:56.940 --> 00:06:00.120
And you can also create a
variety of content including

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:03.480
articles, reports,
presentations, websites, blogs,

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:07.920
and books in a variety of
formats, including HTML, PDF,

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:10.380
Microsoft Word,
epub, and lots more.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:17.100
So let's review our workflow
for today. So we're going to

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:21.480
draw in package download
data from POSit package

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:22.560
manager's API,

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:27.960
